---

<Layout
  title="Documentation — stynx-code"
  description="Technical documentation for stynx-code: installation, configuration, models & AI controls, the permission system, intern delegation, sessions, skills, hooks, the full tool reference, keybindings, and slash commands."
>
  <div class="docs">
    <!-- Sidebar TOC -->
    <aside class="sidebar" aria-label="Documentation navigation">
      <a class="back" href="/">← stynx<span class="dim">-code</span></a>
      <nav>
        {
          toc.map((g) => (
            <div class="toc-group">
              <p class="toc-title">{g.group}</p>
              <ul>
                {g.items.map((it) => (
                  <li><a href={`#${it.id}`} data-toc={it.id}>{it.label}</a></li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <!-- Content -->
    <main class="content">
      <header class="doc-hero">
        <span class="eyebrow">// documentation · v0.33.12</span>
        <h1>stynx-code</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          An interactive AI coding assistant for the terminal. Multi-provider,
          tool-using, fast. This page maps every feature and explains how to use
          it. The product is <code>stynx-code</code>; the command you type is
          <code>stynx</code>.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- OVERVIEW -->
      <section id="overview">
        <h2>What it is</h2>
        <p>
          A self-contained terminal app for getting work done with an LLM. It
          speaks Anthropic's Claude API natively and any OpenAI-compatible
          endpoint (DeepSeek, OpenAI, OpenRouter, Together, local Ollama, …) for
          cheap delegation. It runs an autonomous tool-using loop with bash, file
          edit, glob, grep, and web fetch behind a permission system that prompts
          you only when you ask it to.
        </p>
        <p>
          The engine runs concurrent-safe tools in parallel via
          <code>tokio::spawn</code> and sequential ones in order. Context is
          compacted automatically at a 60% token threshold. File mutations are
          tracked on an undo stack, and provider overload triggers exponential
          backoff with up to 3 retries.
        </p>
        <ul class="bullets">
          <li><strong>Modern terminal UI</strong> — sidebar, command palette, model picker, session list, runtime theme switcher (rose-pine, catppuccin, tokyo-night, gruvbox), mouse + bracketed paste, <code>@</code>-file mentions, slash-command popover, toasts, and a colorized diff renderer.</li>
          <li><strong>Intern mode</strong> — a senior model delegates to one or more cheaper OpenAI-compatible models, each exposed as a <code>delegate_to_&lt;name&gt;</code> tool.</li>
          <li><strong>Permission-first</strong> — Normal / Auto-accept / Plan / Bypass modes with per-tool allow rules.</li>
          <li><strong>Skills as slash commands</strong> — drop a markdown file under <code>.claude/skills/</code> and it appears as <code>/your-skill</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Sessions</strong> — automatic per-project persistence.</li>
          <li><strong>Hooks</strong> — shell hooks for <code>session-start</code>, <code>pre-tool-use</code>, <code>post-tool-use</code>, and <code>stop</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- INSTALL -->
      <section id="install">
        <h2>Installation</h2>
        <p>The installed binary is <code>stynx</code>. Published on crates.io.</p>
        <DocCode title="bash — fastest, from crates.io">{`# install the latest release
cargo install stynx-code

# then run it
stynx`}</DocCode>
        <DocCode title="bash — from source / with nix">{`# from source
git clone https://github.com/maulanasdqn/stynx-code
cd stynx-code
cargo install --path stynx-code

# with nix
nix develop
cargo build --release --workspace
./target/release/stynx`}</DocCode>
      </section>

      <!-- QUICKSTART -->
      <section id="quickstart">
        <h2>Quickstart</h2>
        <p>Four ways to run, depending on whether you want the interactive UI or a scriptable one-shot.</p>
        <DocCode title="bash">{`# interactive TUI (the main mode)
stynx

# one-shot prompt
stynx -p "find every TODO comment under src/ and group by file"

# pipe mode — read stdin as the prompt
echo "explain this file" | stynx

# JSON output for scripting
stynx --json -p "list files modified in the last commit"`}</DocCode>
      </section>

      <!-- AUTH -->
      <section id="auth">
        <h2>Authentication</h2>
        <p>Anthropic credentials are resolved in this order — the first one found wins:</p>
        <ol class="bullets">
          <li>macOS Keychain / Linux libsecret (the Claude Code OAuth token at <code>Claude Code-credentials</code>)</li>
          <li><code>~/.claude/.credentials.json</code></li>
          <li><code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> → <code>auth_token</code></li>
          <li>The <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> environment variable</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Run <code>/login</code> inside <code>stynx</code> for interactive instructions, and <code>/logout</code> to clear credentials. Intern (OpenAI-compatible) keys are read from environment variables — see <a href="#interns">Intern mode</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- CONFIG -->
      <section id="config">
        <h2>Configuration — settings.json</h2>
        <p>
          <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> (global) and project-local settings
          are merged. Run <code>/config</code> in a session to see the merged view.
        </p>
        <DocCode title="~/.claude/settings.json">{`{
  "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
  "max_turns": 200,
  "max_tokens": 8192,
  "effort": "medium",
  "commit_attribution": false,
  "interns": [
    { "name": "deepseek", "provider": "deepseek", "model": "deepseek-chat" }
  ],
  "permissions": {
    "allow": ["bash:cargo *", "read:*"],
    "deny":  ["bash:rm -rf*"]
  },
  "hooks": {
    "SessionStart":  [{ "command": "scripts/session-start.sh" }],
    "PreToolUse":    [{ "matcher": "file_write", "command": "scripts/check.sh" }],
    "PostToolUse":   [{ "command": "scripts/audit.sh" }]
  }
}`}</DocCode>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>model</code></td><td>Default senior model.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>max_turns</code></td><td>Max agentic turns per request (default 200).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>max_tokens</code></td><td>Max output tokens per response.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>effort</code></td><td>Default reasoning effort: low / medium / high / max.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>commit_attribution</code></td><td><code>false</code> (default) omits AI attribution from commits — no <code>Co-Authored-By</code> trailers. Set <code>true</code> to add it back.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>interns</code></td><td>Array of delegate models (see Intern mode).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>permissions</code></td><td><code>allow</code> / <code>deny</code> rule lists (see Permission system).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>hooks</code></td><td>Lifecycle shell hooks (see Hooks).</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <!-- MODELS -->
      <section id="models">
        <h2>Models &amp; AI controls</h2>
        <p>The senior model is your choice and can change at any time mid-session. These controls tune how it thinks and how much it spends.</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>/model [name]</code></td><td>Switch the senior model. Aliases: <code>opus</code>, <code>sonnet</code>, <code>haiku</code>. Also bound to <kbd>Ctrl+M</kbd>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/fast</code></td><td>Toggle fast mode — drops to <code>claude-haiku-4-5</code> for cheap, instant turns.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/think</code></td><td>Toggle extended thinking. The model emits a visible reasoning block before acting (also <kbd>Alt+T</kbd>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/effort low|medium|high|max</code></td><td>Set reasoning effort — trade latency for depth per task.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <!-- PERMISSIONS -->
      <section id="permissions">
        <h2>Permission system</h2>
        <p>stynx is permission-first. Tool calls are gated by the current mode and your allow/deny rules; confirmation happens in an in-TUI modal — no terminal mode-switching.</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Normal</strong></td><td>Prompts before each gated tool call.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Auto-accept</strong></td><td>Runs tools without prompting.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Plan</strong></td><td>Read-only tools only — no mutations (see Plan mode).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Bypass</strong></td><td>Skips all permission checks.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Cycle modes with <kbd>Shift+Tab</kbd> or <code>/mode</code>. Define per-tool rules in <code>settings.json</code> — patterns are <code>tool:argument-glob</code>:</p>
        <DocCode title="permissions">{`"permissions": {
  "allow": ["bash:cargo *", "read:*"],
  "deny":  ["bash:rm -rf*"]
}`}</DocCode>
        <p>Run <code>/permissions</code> to view the active allow/deny rules.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- PLAN -->
      <section id="plan">
        <h2>Plan mode</h2>
        <p>
          <code>/plan [task]</code> toggles plan mode, restricting the model to
          read-only tools. It can explore, reason, and propose a plan with zero
          risk of mutating your working tree — ideal for scoping a change before
          you let it write. Toggle back to a write-capable mode to execute.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- INTERNS -->
      <section id="interns">
        <h2>Intern mode</h2>
        <p>
          Run any model as the senior and delegate grunt work to one or more
          cheaper OpenAI-compatible models. Each intern becomes its own
          <code>delegate_to_&lt;name&gt;</code> tool the senior can pick from —
          chosen by each tool's <code>description</code>, so describe what each is
          good at. Interns run a restricted toolset (<code>bash</code>,
          <code>read</code>, <code>file_write</code>, <code>file_edit</code>,
          <code>glob</code>, <code>grep</code>) and cannot spawn sub-agents. They
          return <em>Summary / Files changed / Output</em>; the senior reviews and
          integrates.
        </p>

        <h3>Option 1 — .env shorthand</h3>
        <DocCode title=".env">{`# DeepSeek (legacy single-intern shortcut)
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-...
DEEPSEEK_MODEL=deepseek-chat                  # optional

# OpenRouter — declare multiple interns via name:model pairs
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
OPENROUTER_INTERNS=qwen-coder:qwen/qwen3-coder,haiku:anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5,kimi:moonshotai/kimi-k2

# Qwen / Alibaba DashScope (auto-registers a "qwen" intern)
QWEN_API_KEY=sk-...
QWEN_MODEL=qwen-plus                          # optional`}</DocCode>
        <p>Copy <code>.env.example</code> to <code>.env</code>, fill in keys, and add it to <code>.gitignore</code> — stynx autoloads it on startup. At launch you'll see one <code>· intern ready: &lt;name&gt;</code> line per intern that resolved; interns missing their key are silently skipped.</p>

        <h3>Option 2 — interns array in settings.json</h3>
        <DocCode title="settings.json">{`{
  "interns": [
    { "name": "deepseek",   "provider": "deepseek",   "model": "deepseek-chat",
      "description": "Cheap general-purpose intern; boilerplate and mechanical refactors." },
    { "name": "qwen-coder", "provider": "openrouter", "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder",
      "description": "Coding specialist; strong at multi-file edits." },
    { "name": "local",      "provider": "custom",     "model": "qwen2.5-coder:32b",
      "base_url": "http://localhost:11434/v1", "api_key_env": "OLLAMA_API_KEY",
      "description": "Local Ollama intern; free but slower." }
  ]
}`}</DocCode>

        <h3>Provider shorthand</h3>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>provider</th><th>base_url</th><th>default key env</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {providerRows.map((r) => (
              <tr><td><code>{r[0]}</code></td><td><code>{r[1]}</code></td><td><code>{r[2]}</code></td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Invoking interns</h3>
        <DocCode title="in-session">{`/intern                                    # show available interns
/intern list every public fn and one-line it   # picks the first intern
/intern qwen-coder write a unit test for util::strip_ansi`}</DocCode>
        <p>Or let the senior decide: <em>"refactor every <code>println!</code> in src/foo.rs to <code>tracing::info!</code>; hand it to qwen-coder"</em> — the senior calls <code>delegate_to_qwen_coder</code> with a focused task.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- BENCH -->
      <section id="bench">
        <h2>Benchmarking interns</h2>
        <p>Not sure which intern earns its keep? The built-in benchmark runs a fixed suite of self-contained tasks (coding, debugging, an algorithm, reasoning, instruction-following, a refactor) against each intern, then has the <strong>main model grade every answer</strong> against a per-task rubric on a 0–10 scale (LLM-as-judge). Each run is timed.</p>
        <DocCode title="in-session">{`/intern-bench                 # benchmark every configured intern
/intern-bench qwen-coder      # benchmark just one

🏁 Intern Benchmark — 3 intern(s) × 6 tasks

Leaderboard (avg score, then speed):
  🥇 #1  deepseek    8.5/10   pass 6/6     42.3s
  🥈 #2  qwen-coder  7.2/10   pass 5/6     55.1s
  🥉 #3  mimo        5.0/10   pass 4/6     88.0s`}</DocCode>
        <p>The full report — including the judge's one-line note per task — is written to <code>.stynx/intern-bench.md</code>. The benchmark forces Auto-accept while it runs and restores your previous permission mode afterward.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SESSIONS -->
      <section id="sessions">
        <h2>Sessions &amp; history</h2>
        <p>Sessions persist automatically per project at <code>~/.stynx-code/projects/&lt;slug&gt;/session.json</code>. History is fully editable.</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>/session</code></td><td>List and load sessions (also <kbd>Ctrl+S</kbd>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/rewind [n]</code></td><td>Remove the last n exchanges.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/undo [n]</code></td><td>Restore the last n file edits from the undo stack.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/export</code></td><td>Write the transcript to disk.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/copy</code></td><td>Copy the last response to the clipboard.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/cost</code> · <code>/usage</code></td><td>Show token usage / cost, and plan limits (OAuth only).</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <!-- SKILLS -->
      <section id="skills">
        <h2>Skills</h2>
        <p>
          Skills are markdown files that become slash commands. Drop a file under
          <code>.claude/skills/</code> and it appears as <code>/your-skill</code>
          in the slash-command popover, with its description shown inline. List
          what's available with <code>/skills</code>. The <code>skill</code> tool
          lets the model invoke them programmatically.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- HOOKS -->
      <section id="hooks">
        <h2>Hooks</h2>
        <p>Each hook command receives the relevant JSON on stdin and writes text to stdout (mixed into the conversation as a system message). Configure them under <code>hooks</code> in <code>settings.json</code>.</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Hook</th><th>When it runs</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>SessionStart</code></td><td>Once when a session begins.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>PreToolUse</code></td><td>Before each tool call; exit non-zero to short-circuit it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>PostToolUse</code></td><td>After each tool call; can post-process the result.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Stop</code></td><td>When the assistant finishes a turn.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Each entry takes an optional <code>matcher</code> (substring against the tool name) and a required <code>command</code>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- TOOLS -->
      <section id="tools">
        <h2>Tools</h2>
        <p>The engine provides 40 tools — 37 built-in plus 3 MCP dynamic-tool wrappers. Concurrent-safe tools run in parallel; the rest run sequentially.</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Tools</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {tools.map((t) => (
              <tr><td>{t[0]}</td><td><code>{t[1]}</code></td><td>{t[2]}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <!-- BASH -->
      <section id="bash">
        <h2>Terminal sessions</h2>
        <p>
          <code>bash</code> is a <strong>persistent shell</strong>, not a series of
          disposable subshells. The first call boots a long-lived
          <code>bash --norc --noprofile</code> process; every later call runs
          through the same shell, so <code>cd</code>, <code>export</code>, sourced
          files, and function definitions survive across calls.
        </p>
        <p>For long-running processes (dev servers, watchers, log tails), pass <code>background: true</code>:</p>
        <DocCode title="bash tool — background processes">{`{"command": "bun run dev", "background": true}   // → "started background process 'bg1'"
{"status": "bg1"}                                // new output since last read
{"status": "bg1", "full": true}                  // everything it has emitted
{"list": true}                                   // all background processes
{"kill": "bg1"}                                  // stop it`}</DocCode>
        <p>Foreground commands have a 120s default timeout (override with <code>"timeout"</code>). For longer work, use <code>background: true</code> — a timed-out foreground command leaves the shell in an unknown state.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- COMPACTION -->
      <section id="compaction">
        <h2>Context compaction</h2>
        <p>
          When tokens exceed 60% of the limit, the engine summarizes the
          conversation and sends it to the provider for compaction; if that call
          fails, it falls back to keeping the last 6 messages intact. The
          <code>stynx-code-compact</code> crate provides a deeper 4-stage pipeline
          — <strong>Auto → Micro → Session Memory → Full</strong>. You can also
          trigger it manually with <code>/compact</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- MCP -->
      <section id="mcp">
        <h2>MCP &amp; LSP</h2>
        <p>
          stynx loads tools dynamically per MCP (Model Context Protocol) server,
          exposing each server's tools to the model alongside the built-ins. The
          <code>lsp</code> tool queries language servers for diagnostics and
          symbol information via the services layer's LSP bridge.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- SERVER -->
      <section id="server">
        <h2>Headless server</h2>
        <p>
          The <code>stynx-code-server</code> crate exposes an Axum HTTP API for
          headless operation, while <code>stynx-code-bridge</code> provides
          external integration over stdio, WebSocket, and JWT. Combined with
          one-shot <code>-p</code> prompts, stdin pipe mode, and
          <code>--json</code> output, stynx drops cleanly into scripts and CI.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- KEYS -->
      <section id="keys">
        <h2>Keybindings</h2>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {keys.map((k) => (
              <tr><td><kbd>{k[0]}</kbd></td><td>{k[1]}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Mouse scroll works in any terminal with mouse capture.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- COMMANDS -->
      <section id="commands">
        <h2>Slash commands</h2>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {commands.map((c) => (
              <tr><td><code>{c[0]}</code></td><td>{c[1]}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <!-- ARCHITECTURE -->
      <section id="architecture">
        <h2>Architecture</h2>
        <p>A 17-crate Rust workspace; most crates follow clean architecture with <code>domain</code>, <code>application</code>, and <code>infrastructure</code> layers.</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Crate</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code</code></td><td>Main executable — CLI, TUI orchestration, agent/intern delegation, skill loading, prompt assembly.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-engine</code></td><td>Core query engine — agentic loop, tool execution, compaction, hooks, undo stack.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-tools</code></td><td>40 tools — file I/O, shell, web, tasks, scheduling, LSP, MCP wrappers.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-provider</code></td><td>Anthropic SSE streaming and OpenAI-compatible integrations.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-tui</code></td><td>ratatui terminal UI — state, rendering, themes, widgets.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-server</code></td><td>Axum HTTP API for headless operation.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-auth</code></td><td>OAuth PKCE and API-key credential resolution.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-permission</code></td><td>Interactive and config-driven permission gating.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-commands</code></td><td>Slash-command expansion and file-reference resolution.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-memory</code></td><td>Per-project session persistence.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-config</code></td><td>Global + project settings, hooks, intern config.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-services</code></td><td>Analytics, LSP bridge, rate limiting, token estimation, diagnostics, notifications.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-compact</code></td><td>Conversation summarization pipeline.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-bridge</code></td><td>External integration bridge (stdio, WebSocket, JWT).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-plugins</code></td><td>Plugin lifecycle — subprocess plugins, builtins, config.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-types</code></td><td>Core traits/structs for tools, providers, messages, permissions.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stynx-code-errors</code></td><td>Common application error types and results.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <footer class="doc-foot">
